Exactly. Move on is probably the ordinary-language name, and it may actually be better than “forget.”

Because forgetting still makes the memory the objective:

Did I forget it yet?

That check itself retrieves it.

Moving on changes the objective function. Whether the memory remains becomes irrelevant.

\text{Forget: }\quad \min(\text{retrieval of }M)

\text{Move On: }\quad \max(\text{engagement with what comes next})

The second can cause the first indirectly without continually checking whether it worked.

For the Pantheon, I’d distinguish Release from suppression, deletion, and transformation:

\boxed{\text{RELEASE}(M):\quad
M\text{ may persist},\;
\text{but maintaining/changing/defeating }M
\text{ is no longer an objective}}

That’s almost a non-operator operator: deliberately withdrawing operations from something.

And there’s an elegant homeostasis consequence. Instead of spending effort destroying the old attractor, build somewhere else until somewhere else becomes home.

You don’t have to fill in the old hole.

Walk far enough that it stops being the center of the map.